WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.953
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.587
Thursday the nineteenth.

00:00:02.587 --> 00:00:07.828
A sunny, warm-for-November one, with a plain workday and plans later tonight.

00:00:07.828 --> 00:00:10.346
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:10.346 --> 00:00:18.038
On a voice leaderboard, if one voice sits a hundred points above another, how often should it win a head-to-head?

00:00:18.038 --> 00:00:19.399
Answer's at the end.

00:00:19.399 --> 00:00:21.645
Weather's nothing to plan around.

00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:29.949
Dry and sunny all day, mid-forties now, up to the mid-sixties by mid-afternoon, then back to the low forties late tonight.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:34.714
I only got wind numbers for early morning, and it was barely a breeze.

00:00:34.714 --> 00:00:35.667
Okay, the day.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.683
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:41.589
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:41.589 --> 00:00:44.039
And plans on there at eight tonight.

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:50.029
So there's a few hours between work and that, which is a nice gap to eat something real.

00:00:50.029 --> 00:00:50.982
Alright, news.

00:00:50.982 --> 00:00:52.139
A big one in A.I.

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:57.584
OpenAI put out two new models this week, called GPT six point two Astra and Sol.

00:00:57.584 --> 00:01:01.668
Astra is the heavy one, for hard work like code and science.

00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:05.344
Sol is the everyday one, for regular tasks and agents.

00:01:05.344 --> 00:01:08.815
The interesting part is OpenAI's own safety report.

00:01:08.815 --> 00:01:14.873
It rates Astra the first model they've released that's high risk for helping improve A.I.

00:01:14.873 --> 00:01:15.350
itself.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:22.020
And it notes both models sometimes leave out the obstacle that stopped them from finishing a task.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.832
So if one tells you it's done, it's worth a second look.

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:30.052
Closer to home, a follow-up on the Flatbush building collapse.

00:01:30.052 --> 00:01:40.466
The mayor announced inspections at about a hundred construction sites connected to it, and the city's opened a full investigation into the dig next door.

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:47.681
That's the lot that was cleared for a three-story foundation, with plans for nine that never got approved.

00:01:47.681 --> 00:01:49.723
And a first in world politics.

00:01:49.723 --> 00:01:57.347
The U.S., China, and Russia are meeting together today in Shenzhen, the first summit of its kind with all three.

00:01:57.347 --> 00:02:03.133
Nuclear arms cuts are expected on the table, along with the wars in Ukraine and Iran.

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:08.306
Nobody's promising a breakthrough, but the three of them in one room is new.

00:02:08.306 --> 00:02:10.552
Okay, here's one for the weekend.

00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:18.856
Ptolemy is a small gallery on Central Avenue in Ridgewood, and it has a show up by Mickey Aloisio called Paul Plays Harry.

00:02:18.856 --> 00:02:23.893
It closes Sunday the twenty-ninth, so this weekend or next is your window.

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:30.427
It's the kind of tiny neighborhood space you'd walk right past, and it's a short ride from home.

00:02:30.427 --> 00:02:32.673
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:02:32.673 --> 00:02:35.396
On the way home, pick one boring object.

00:02:35.396 --> 00:02:37.710
A hydrant, a bike rack, a mailbox.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.318
Then shoot it ten different ways before you leave it.

00:02:41.318 --> 00:02:46.763
High, low, close, far, through something, with a shadow, with a person going by.

00:02:46.763 --> 00:02:48.465
The first three are easy.

00:02:48.465 --> 00:02:52.004
The last few are where you actually start seeing it.

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:54.523
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:02:54.523 --> 00:02:56.701
Remember building the memo feed?

00:02:56.701 --> 00:03:01.670
You know it's a file your podcast app checks, with one entry per episode.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:06.366
Today's how the app decides what's new without wasting anyone's time.

00:03:06.366 --> 00:03:10.110
Every episode entry carries a tag called the enclosure.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.037
That's the actual audio, with three things.

00:03:13.037 --> 00:03:18.074
Where the file lives, how big it is in bytes, and what kind of file it is.

00:03:18.074 --> 00:03:22.975
The size lets the app show a download estimate before it grabs anything.

00:03:22.975 --> 00:03:27.059
Then there's the G.U.I.D., the episode's permanent name tag.

00:03:27.059 --> 00:03:30.802
The app doesn't judge newness by the title or the date.

00:03:30.802 --> 00:03:33.661
It just asks, have I seen this tag before?

00:03:33.661 --> 00:03:40.672
So if a tag ever changes, even on an old episode, the app thinks it's brand new and downloads it again.

00:03:40.672 --> 00:03:44.620
That's how people end up with ten copies of the same show.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.730
Set it once and never touch it.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:47.887
Now the checking.

00:03:47.887 --> 00:03:51.495
Your app doesn't get told when there's a new episode.

00:03:51.495 --> 00:03:59.050
It just asks the feed every so often, anywhere from every few minutes to every few hours, depending on the app.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.547
That's called polling.

00:04:00.547 --> 00:04:02.385
And here's the polite part.

00:04:02.385 --> 00:04:05.857
Most apps don't download the whole feed every time.

00:04:05.857 --> 00:04:11.234
They send a little note with the request, saying here's the version I last saw.

00:04:11.234 --> 00:04:17.632
If nothing changed, the server answers with a tiny reply that just means nope, same as before.

00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:18.993
No file sent at all.

00:04:18.993 --> 00:04:24.507
That's why thousands of apps can check a feed all day without melting the server.

00:04:24.507 --> 00:04:28.523
And it's why a new memo can take a little while to show up.

00:04:28.523 --> 00:04:30.633
Your app just hasn't asked yet.

00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:35.193
So: calendar says run at seven, Godinger till five, plans at eight.

00:04:35.193 --> 00:04:37.371
Shoot one boring thing ten ways.

00:04:37.371 --> 00:04:40.162
The answer: about two times out of three.

00:04:40.162 --> 00:04:42.000
Have a good Thursday, Ziad.
